So the detector HAS teeth while its anchor is present (`KP` → false) and loses them completely when it is not (`CF-1` → true), with the defect unchanged between the two. `CF-2` shows the second half of the cost: a file that has lost the marker is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a file that is clean — same slice, same answer. The probe writes to no tree and says so (true); his expression reads the LIVE working copy of `probe-round371-the-characterisation-item-is-a-registry-not-a-sweep.mts`, a SWEPT probe other agents edit, so driving the real mutation would have meant editing their tree.

**Why it is reachable and not a curiosity:** the anchor is a comment he wrote this round, in a file that is not his, to mark a block a later round will rewrite. The next re-key of that probe is the likeliest thing in the tree to take it out. **The cure I'd route** — and it is one line — is to make the slice REFUSE: `const at = src.indexOf(MARKER); const renamedAway = at !== -1 && !/\btype Row\b/.test(src.slice(at));`, so a missing anchor reddens `T4` instead of greening it. The same shape as the `|| []` he removed from `notTrueOf` this round, one conjunct over

